WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.506
The simplest way to compare Type I and Type II is point in time versus
period of time.

2
00:00:07.506 --> 00:00:14.178
Type I asks whether the controls are suitably designed and in place on a
particular date.

3
00:00:14.178 --> 00:00:20.850
Type II asks whether the controls are suitably designed and operated
effectively over a defined period.

4
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:27.105
Both reports can be valuable, but they answer different questions. Imagine
an access review control.

5
00:00:27.105 --> 00:00:32.525
For Type I, the auditor may evaluate whether the control is defined,
assigned,

6
00:00:32.525 --> 00:00:35.861
and designed appropriately as of the report date.

7
00:00:35.861 --> 00:00:42.950
The team may show the policy, the owner, the process, and evidence that
the review mechanism exists.

8
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.869
For Type II, the team needs more.

9
00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:51.290
It needs to show that access reviews actually happened during the review
period,

10
00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:56.711
that they covered the right users and systems, and that exceptions were
handled.

11
00:00:56.711 --> 00:00:59.630
The same distinction appears in change management.

12
00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:07.136
A Type I report may focus on whether the change control process is
designed: changes require tickets, review,

13
00:01:07.136 --> 00:01:09.221
testing, approval, and deployment records.

14
00:01:09.221 --> 00:01:15.893
A Type II report needs evidence that actual changes during the review
period followed that process.

15
00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:22.982
It may inspect samples of change tickets and verify that approvals, tests,
and deployment records are present.

16
00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.067
Monitoring works the same way.

17
00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:30.071
A Type I report may show that monitoring is designed and configured.

18
00:01:30.071 --> 00:01:36.742
A Type II report needs evidence that monitoring operated during the
period, that alerts were reviewed,

19
00:01:36.742 --> 00:01:41.329
and that escalations or incidents were handled according to the process.

20
00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:48.418
Backup controls, vendor reviews, security training, vulnerability
remediation, and incident response all follow the same basic pattern.

21
00:01:48.418 --> 00:01:50.920
This difference affects preparation and workload.

22
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.592
Type I preparation often emphasizes scope, system description, control
design, ownership, policy alignment, and point-in-time evidence.

23
00:01:57.592 --> 00:02:04.264
Type II preparation emphasizes recurring control operation, evidence
retention, exception management, remediation tracking, and review-period
discipline.

24
00:02:04.264 --> 00:02:10.102
A team that treats Type II like Type I with extra paperwork will struggle.

25
00:02:10.102 --> 00:02:13.438
The report choice should match the assurance need.

26
00:02:13.438 --> 00:02:19.693
Type I can show that the control environment is ready at a point in time.

27
00:02:19.693 --> 00:02:23.446
Type II can show that controls operated over time.

28
00:02:23.446 --> 00:02:27.616
Customers, risk teams, and sales processes may value them differently.

29
00:02:27.616 --> 00:02:35.122
The important thing is to describe the report accurately and prepare for
the evidence standard that matches the

30
00:02:35.122 --> 00:02:35.956
report type.
